# {{PROJECT_NAME}} branch integration and cleanup

## Required MR pipeline monitoring owner

Every required GitLab MR pipeline has one explicitly named monitoring owner,
normally the coordinator. Record the owner with the MR/pipeline ID and immutable
source SHA when the MR is opened or the pipeline is created. The owner verifies
the expected pipeline actually started, tracks it to a terminal outcome, reports
the result and routes the first failure for diagnosis. The Claude session-handoff
watcher observes Claude sessions; it does not inspect GitLab MRs, pipelines or
jobs. Auto-merge, a review request or a watcher notice does not prove active
monitoring. If ownership changes, record a direct handoff and acknowledgment;
never leave a required pipeline implicitly owned. Keep updates event-driven and
concise, with no repetitive unchanged status reports. This adds no review, CI,
merge or release authority.

## Post-merge qualification runs locally

Default sequence for implementation MRs: focused checks and current hosted fast/supervisor/Duo review → authorized merge → full local qualification on the exact merged integration SHA. The existing implementer coordinator must automatically launch, own and track that full-profile run; no extra owner command, new persistent coordinator or full pre-merge gate. Preserve immutable inputs, real logs/exit/timings, complete failure disposition and cleanup. A busy local harness queues the batch visibly; it must not silently disappear or overlap conflicting resources.

Automatic integration/release-branch post-merge and tag-push pipelines are disabled, including lightweight checks. Feature MRs retain hosted fast checks; owner-selected RC promotion MRs require complete hosted release qualification. Preserve separately authorized diagnostic/provider schedule/web/API runs. Final Goal ACCEPT remains pending until local full coverage and other Goal gates clear. Ready authorized successor implementation may overlap the local run after predecessor merge, in isolation with shared locks respected. Predecessor final ACCEPT gates successor integration/final acceptance; its own owner retains repairs. Preserve explicit phase/owner holds, documentation-only suppression and low-risk exemptions. This replaces the earlier pre-merge/unmerged-successor default.

## Owner mandate and stop bounds

Check [mandates and stops](../../.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md#bounded-specification-authority-and-owner-stop-boundaries)
before automatic specification/planning/release. Missing Specs need bounded
owner authority; ready Specs/approved Plans never override a stop. No successor
lookahead during endpoint CI/closeout; clarify ambiguous boundaries before crossing.

## Automatic next-task planning

When the predecessor implementation MR first opens against `{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}`
(drafts included), start provisional planning for the
earliest eligible task with an existing ready Goal/Spec, using the verified
coordinator and one planner. Record baseline/holds; do not wait for predecessor CI/Duo, merge,
acceptance or cleanup merely to plan. Integration is fallback discovery only. No implementation,
shared test-stack work or bypass of explicit holds. Follow
[planning policy](../../.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md#automatic-planning-after-integration).

## Automatic execution release

After reviewed predecessor source integration, reassess the provisional Plan and
record/relay release once Plan/input/scope, other dependency, owner-decision,
budget and resource start gates close. Implement on an isolated task branch while
predecessor staging CI runs; final predecessor ACCEPT gates successor integration
and final acceptance. Pause affected work for unresolved security/contracts or
failed qualification. Predecessor implementation owners retain their staging
repairs/ledger, in parallel with successor implementation; preserve one writer.
Explicit holds/barriers remain. No extra routine owner prompt. See
[release policy](../../.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md#automatic-reassessment-and-execution-release).

## Mechanical repair during an approved task

Fix in-scope selector/fixture/pin/catalog parity and harness/import bookkeeping
without another approval round under [standing authority](../../.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md#autonomous-mechanical-corrections).
Keep meaningful assertions, exact scope, focused checks and Result evidence.
Do not expand production behavior/constraints, skip CI or change merge gates.

## Before every local batch

1. `git fetch origin`
2. Assess the exact target delta for validation and execution-authority impact. Rebase/reconcile relevant or
   unknown changes, preserving dirty work and bindings. If demonstrably irrelevant to both, record the compared
   SHAs/reason and defer rebase/input reconstruction. Always reconcile before final qualification and MR opening;
   never rewrite protected branches.
3. Environment check: {{PRE_BATCH_ENV_CHECK}}. If it fails, do not launch.
4. Record target and candidate SHAs and binding provenance.

Fast checks: `{{FAST_CHECK_COMMAND}}`. Full qualification: `{{FULL_CHECK_COMMAND}}`. Batches finish, fix, rerun,
advance; manual feedback precedes final qualification. With the default `local-after-merge` placement, the full local
suite runs on the exact merged integration SHA; it is not a full pre-MR gate. Follow the explicitly configured
placement when the adopting owner selects an alternative.

## Final validation

Placement: `{{FINAL_VALIDATION_PLACEMENT}}`. Canceled post-merge pipeline satisfies the gate:
`{{CANCELED_PIPELINE_SATISFIES}}` (canceled is recorded as canceled, never as passing; a failed job always fails;
pending or running never qualifies).

## Feature and Goal branches

1. Push safekeeping checkpoints to the temporary branch after each round.
2. After {{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}}'s pre-integration review, re-check the target, rebase if needed and rerun only
   affected checks.
3. Open one merge request to `{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}` with source-branch deletion enabled, using `{{MR_TOOL}}`.
4. Inspect automated review findings and unresolved threads before merging.
5. Merge only with owner authorization; record conditional integration and the post-merge disposition.
6. Verify the source branch is deleted; delete only verified merged temporary branches.

At Goal acceptance, audit all task-owned remote refs with `git ls-remote --heads`;
the provider's source-branch deletion setting is not proof that every task branch
was cleaned up. Verify MR state, ownership and integration/preservation of unique
content before deletion. Check local refs and worktrees separately because remote
deletion leaves local branches behind. Preserve unclear or unique work with a
recorded reason. Clean only exact task-owned Docker image IDs; retain shared images
and never use host-wide prune.

## GitLab/glab commands (when the provider is gitlab)

```sh
glab mr list --source-branch TEMP_BRANCH --target-branch {{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}
glab mr create --source-branch TEMP_BRANCH --target-branch {{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}} --title "TITLE" \
  --description "Evidence: see the Goal Result." --remove-source-branch --yes
glab mr view MR_IID --comments
glab mr merge MR_IID
```

Promotion from `{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}` to `{{RELEASE_BRANCH}}` uses a separate merge request with source-branch
deletion **off**.

If the owner selects [RC promotion qualification](../../.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#owner-controlled-rc-promotion-option), the coordinator instead creates an internal temporary branch pinned to the requested RC tag and opens its MR to `{{RELEASE_BRANCH}}`. The MR runs required full hosted qualification; RC/final tag and release-branch pushes start no duplicate pipeline. After success, wait for explicit owner promotion instruction, verify actual qualified content, and delete only that temporary source branch. Keep `{{INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}`, `{{RELEASE_BRANCH}}` and RC tags. The owner separately decides when to tag the verified release branch. Any owner waiver of automated review applies only to that promotion, not ordinary feature MRs. Implement and verify the provider adapter before using this option; the owner does not manage the temporary branch.

## Closeout

Verify integrated content, unique work and live ownership before `git worktree remove`; close disposable merge
requests; archive session records before pruning; never delete a live coordinator's directory.


Apply [validation economy and failure handling](../../.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#validation-economy-and-recurring-failures)
and the [routine closeout receipt](../../.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/closeout-risks.md#routine-closeout-receipt).
Continue authorized review/MR/merge/closeout transitions without repeat proceed prompts;
keep native permissions, owner limits and required qualification gates.


## Mandatory task-acceptance cleanup — owner direction, 2026-10-17

At every final task ACCEPT, immediately remove all finished task-owned worktrees
and temporary resources under the [mandatory cleanup policy](../../.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/closeout-risks.md#mandatory-cleanup-at-every-task-acceptance).
Unlock finished agent-tool worktrees; preserve unique commits and dirty/untracked
work before force removal when needed. Clean task-owned containers, unused images
(including attributable untagged IDs), volumes and disposable evidence without
another routine approval. Keep active coordinator/successor/preview resources and
specific recorded retention/native-permission holds. Verify a compact receipt;
no silent lock/dirty-work deferral, blanket host prune or extra test campaign.


## Repair completeness before integration

Follow [complete repair inventory and supervisor verification](../../.agents/skills/restruct-sdlc/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#complete-repair-inventory-and-supervisor-verification).
Inventory all unresolved failures across the task's failed runs in its existing
Result, map corrections/dispositions and verify focused attributable proof before
merge. The supervisor independently checks completeness against actual logs;
later passing subsets do not erase earlier failures. Use the existing pre-merge
integration job only when local proof is unavailable and broader execution is
necessary. One evidenced job retry stays bounded; no duplicate tracker/full-suite
default, inferred acceptance or permission bypass.


## Owner-selected RC auto-merge — 2026-10-19

The owner may explicitly enable auto-merge for a named RC promotion MR or a clearly recorded scope of RC MRs. Manual promotion remains the default when no such instruction exists. This owner instruction authorizes queuing promotion before CI finishes; once every required full-qualification job succeeds, the provider may merge without another owner prompt. Record the owner instruction, MR, immutable source SHA and selected mode. Before arming, verify the tag/source identity, required complete pipeline, non-squash merge, and conflict-free prospective target tree equal to the RC tree; source-pin the request. Preserve target-movement monitoring and post-merge parent/tree verification without claiming an atomic target lock. Retain and independently verify full project/pipeline/job/artifact proof before claiming qualified release. No failed, skipped, optional or partial coverage counts as qualification. Do not fabricate manual-mode proof or change API snapshots to satisfy a verifier. A manual-only helper's refusal of queued/merged MRs is a mode limitation, not a passing auto-mode proof; use truthful independent verification or a separately reviewed mode-aware adapter. Final release tagging remains a separate owner decision. Feature-MR auto-merge defaults alone never authorize an RC-to-release merge.


## Optional RC Duo review and planned follow-ups — 2026-10-19

RC promotion MRs may use Duo as optional advisory review; Duo is not a required promotion approval gate. Triage every actionable finding and record its disposition in the existing release Result/review. Changes arising from that review must be scheduled and planned as bounded follow-up work: identify the responsible Goal/task or backlog item, intended milestone, scope, acceptance criteria and required focused validation under normal authority. Do not leave actionable recommendations only in an MR comment or silently claim them fixed. Do not change the code or move/recreate an existing RC tag to satisfy a review. If a follow-up changes the release candidate or must ship before promotion, integrate it through the normal staging workflow, then cut and fully qualify a new RC tag under the applicable release authorization. The owner may instead retain a documented prerelease follow-up while the current RC proceeds. Preserve ordinary feature-MR Duo requirements, required full RC CI, security/owner dispositions, selected auto/manual merge mode and the separate final-tag decision. This policy creates no automatic RC2, unbounded repair mandate or new tracker.


## Owner-waived canceled RC qualification — 2026-10-19

The owner may explicitly cancel a named RC promotion MR's pipeline and direct that cancellation to satisfy the current RC promotion gate, treating it as success for the workflow decision only. Record an owner-authorized qualification waiver with the RC/tag/source SHA, MR/pipeline IDs, actual canceled/partial/failed job states, any known findings and the owner's instruction. Never report the canceled pipeline or unexecuted tests as PASS, fabricate green evidence, erase prior failures or claim complete qualification. This exception is specific to the named candidate/pipeline; it is not the default cancellation policy for feature MRs or other RCs. Schedule a follow-up immutable RC tag to run the complete required suite, carrying forward unresolved findings and the qualification debt in the existing release Result/backlog/Plan. The follow-up RC's full suite is required unless the owner separately changes that instruction. The owner may choose this waiver alongside the recorded auto/manual promotion mode; provider auto-merge still depends on the provider's actual cancellation behavior. If existing provider rules prevent merging a canceled pipeline, report that concrete limitation rather than changing project-wide protections, manufacturing a success job or treating a queue as a completed merge. No pipeline is canceled merely because this option is documented, and final release tagging remains separately owner-controlled.


## RC source selection at cut time

For an owner-authorized RC cut, fetch the configured integration branch and tag
its latest remote commit observed at cut time (using the adopting repository’s configured branch). Do not
require an older acceptance/review SHA or exclude later integrated documentation
or implementation merely because it arrived after the cut was requested. No
additional owner confirmation or documentation-only ancestry check is needed
solely because staging advanced. An explicitly owner-pinned source or release
content exclusion still takes precedence.

Record the selected full commit SHA/tree and create the immutable annotated RC
tag there. A concurrent later staging push belongs to a later candidate; never
move the tag to chase it. The promotion branch/MR and required full qualification
use that exact tagged source. This source-selection rule does not accept pending
Goals, waive findings/CI, authorize an unrequested RC/main merge/final tag or
change the selected promotion mode. Existing tags and pipelines stay unchanged.

## Descriptive tag and release notes

Keep tag names canonical SemVer. Every new annotated tag message and GitLab
Release entry lists included Goal/task IDs and short titles, material changes
since the preceding candidate/release, and the exact source commit. Final
release notes summarize delivered scope since the previous stable release and
state qualification truthfully; never imply planned or unaccepted work shipped.
Published tags are immutable: improve an existing GitLab Release entry rather
than moving or recreating a tag.
